      en: "Rabat is the capital city of Morocco and one of its four historic imperial cities, located at the mouth of the Bou-Regreg River on the Atlantic coast. The city serves as the political and administrative heart of the kingdom, elegantly blending its medieval history with modern urban planning. Key landmarks include the Hassan Tower, a massive unfinished minaret from the 12th century, and the adjacent Mausoleum of Mohammed V, which showcases exquisite traditional craftsmanship. The Kasbah of the Udayas, a fortified district overlooking the sea, is famous for its blue-and-white alleys and serene Andalusian gardens. Rabat was designated as a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2012, recognized for the successful integration of its French colonial legacy and traditional Islamic heritage. As the home of the Royal Palace and numerous foreign embassies, the city maintains a sophisticated and relatively calm atmosphere compared to other Moroccan cities. Significant recent developments, such as the Grand Theatre of Rabat and high-speed rail connections, underscore its role as a bridge between Morocco's past and its future. The city's clean boulevards, extensive green spaces, and cultural institutions make it a premier destination for diplomacy and tourism alike.",
